## 4. The half-field tradeoff

High-NA does not simply replace every existing EUV tool.

Because the 0.55-NA projection optics are so large, ASML uses **anamorphic optics**: the image is reduced differently in the two directions.

That lets chipmakers keep using conventionally sized reticles, but the wafer exposure field becomes **half the area** of the normal 0.33-NA EUV field.

Low-NA EUV can expose about **26 mm × 33 mm** in one field.

High-NA’s field is roughly **26 mm × 16.5 mm**.

For many structures this is manageable.

For very large dies, especially AI accelerators near the conventional reticle limit, the smaller field can create another integration problem: some layers may need stitching across fields or a carefully chosen mix of High-NA and Low-NA exposures.

Better resolution can therefore create a new geometric constraint.

## 5. Why productivity still matters

A smaller field means the scanner must expose more fields to cover the same wafer area.

If nothing else changed, that would hurt throughput.

ASML therefore redesigned the EXE platform with faster wafer and reticle stages.

This is a useful fab-operator lesson:

**a better imaging specification is not enough if the machine gives up too much wafer productivity to achieve it.**

The tool still has to deliver competitive wafers per hour, overlay, dose efficiency and availability.

That is why High-NA is not merely an optics project. It is a whole-system engineering problem.

## 6. The cost hurdle is enormous

High-NA machines are among the most expensive manufacturing tools ever built.

Public reporting has put first-generation systems around the **€350 million / roughly $400 million** class.

That scale matters, but the tool’s sticker price is not the final economic answer.

A €350 million tool that removes several masks and repeated process passes across a high-volume node may create attractive system economics.

The same tool used on a layer that 0.33-NA EUV already prints cheaply and reliably may add little value.

So High-NA adoption should be **layer-specific and node-specific**, not automatic.

## 7. Maturity matters as much as resolution

A mature 0.33-NA EUV fleet has years of production learning behind it.

High-NA must prove:

- imaging and overlay;
- resist behavior;
- mask quality;
- defectivity;
- tool availability;
- throughput at real production doses;
- matching between machines;
- process integration across the fab.

By early 2026, ASML said High-NA had reached readiness for high-volume chip production after extensive wafer processing.

But customers still have to qualify it inside their own nodes and products.

That distinction matters:

**technology readiness is not the same as customer production maturity.**

A foundry can rationally delay High-NA on a given layer if the older flow is already qualified, yielding well and economically competitive.

